ast sits at a coin-flip margin tilted marginally toward Republicans.", "watch": "The first general-election polls, each party's eventual nominee, and North Carolina's push for an early 2028 primary could quickly reshape the picture.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Carolina sits near the center of the presidential map, with a partisan lean that favors Republicans by about a point. The incumbent president is term-limited, so both parties will nominate fresh candidates, and no fundraising figures, polls, or expert ratings exist yet for the general election. That leaves fundamentals to carry the forecast: a slight Republican edge on the state's underlying lean.\n\nWith no polls to weigh and no raters to consult, the race rates as a genuine tossup, tilting marginally toward Republicans. Uncertainty is appropriately wide for an open contest this early with no direct measurement of voter preference. Both parties' primary battles will shape the eventual matchup, and North Carolina's bid for the first presidential primary slot could raise the state's profile and the volume of campaigning there.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4508, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4639, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4659, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4636, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4637, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4642, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4667, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.4617, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.4598, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.4577, "margin": -1.0}, {"d